{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact 3d1223a1e06271fe6911598a716379de653e1fd6 for fork-token request git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:02836f9a6a94cea345d675a09b021dfbba1206c2 (#9477). All 15 exact-head artifacts were independently inspected as effective and directly on builder promise git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:95e84afef5a456c833de85de0eb18ada9f0c9fd0. plans/README.md is primary.\n\nTwo implementation defects and two ownership-condition failures prevent approval. The requester has since confirmed the stronger conditions in #10806, subject to the provider/settlement correction below. Four independent controls fail at runtime on the unmodified candidate; these are not compile or setup failures.\n\n1. P1 — The new fork token check preserves the live clock-skew failure in the other half of pinObjects.\npackages/git/src/workspace/fork-tokens.ts@3d1223a1:388-406 compares provider expiry with the Room's arrival time plus the requested lifetime, with no allowance. Independent C4 supplies an otherwise valid read-token answer whose provider expiry is only 67 ms ahead. It is refused as \"an expiry later than the lifetime asked\", becomes owed, and the caller never reaches its pin. That is the same skew simulated by the new live503 regression in packages/room/test/workerd/live-propose-df6ff8d3.test.ts@6e585cf9. The pending live503 head changes canonical MintLedger only; merging it with this fork head will leave the fork half rejecting that answer. I have accepted that other review but have not yet approved its proposed allowance or verified its live report. Reconcile the two lanes against the reviewed clock/expiry contract and prove the composed Room propose/land path with the provider clock ahead. Retain excessive-expiry refusal and any absolute-deadline bounds; do not just remove validation. C4 is a synthetic reproduction, not an independent measurement of the live provider.\n\n2. P2 — A workspace sweep can revoke a token that pinning actively holds.\npackages/git/src/workspace/workspaces.ts@3d1223a1:649-661 builds the keep set once and then awaits revocations. Independent C3 starts a mint whose token has applied but whose answer is held; the sweep lists it and waits on revoking an earlier token. While that wait is outstanding, the mint answers, its row becomes held, and pinning gets the token. The sweep resumes using its old keep set and revokes that now-held token. This contradicts the new held-token exemption and can break an active pin. F16 only starts its sweep after the token is already held and misses this interleaving. Recheck current ownership at the destructive boundary and coordinate the sweep/mint transition so an earlier snapshot is not authority to revoke. Keep the sweep's legitimate cleanup and bounds. A temporary point lookup immediately before revocation made C3 pass; the source was then restored and C3 failed again. That is a diagnostic control, not a proposed production patch or proof of every race.\n\n3. P2 — Resolve the request's no-unattributed-revocation condition across the actual workspace owner.\nThe request expressly says never to revoke an unattributed token. The report's R-MINT-5 analogy and \"Choices for the checker\" exempt Workspaces' existing R-WS-3 sweep. C1 uses the real Workspaces owner: a create applies and loses its answer; the fork ledger has one unknown row with no token ID; ending the lease invokes the workspace sweep, which revokes that token by its inventory ID anyway. This is not a newly discovered property hidden from the report, nor a claim that R-WS-3 lacks prior authority: it is a concrete mismatch between this delivery and this request's unqualified condition. The F4/R1 isolated-ledger controls do not establish the requested property for the composed owner. Resolve the two rules explicitly with the requester and implement/test the resulting coherent ownership contract. Do not silently narrow the request to only ForkTokens.observe, or remove workspace security cleanup and declare success. Any deliberate change to a condition or authority must use the workroom's appropriate request/decision process, not this review's approval.\n\n4. P2 — Resolve the known-ID failure guarantee at the emergency-revocation boundary.\npackages/git/src/workspace/fork-tokens.ts@3d1223a1:365-369 and 466-477 forget the answer's ID when recording it fails and the immediate revocation also fails. Independent C2 injects exactly one transient write failure at sent-to-held; storage is healthy afterwards, but the failed emergency revocation leaves a sent row with no ID. Restart turns it unknown, and a successful later alarm observes instead of retrying the now-known token. The token remains active. F21 deliberately expects this earlier-state outcome. R-MINT-3 permits an earlier state after a failed write, so I am not claiming this is a simple violation of that clause; however the fork request separately requires failed revocations to remain owed by ID, and the delivery's broad failed-revocation guarantee is not true on this path. Preserve the answered ID as a durable owed duty when storage can accept it, with bounded retry/restart tests, or obtain an explicit durable resolution of the weaker guarantee. Never pretend durable recovery can be guaranteed while storage permanently refuses all writes. Do not settle an actually unknown request by time or inventory to hide this gap.\n\nPost-invitation head news: I inspected the effective planner assertion git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:66f8b350328d0efdaca791ea0c32f7b6427d372e (#10806) in full and acknowledge it. It rejects the report's sweep exemption, confirms that received IDs must be made durable and failed revocations remain owed by ID, and agrees that the stale keep-set race must be fixed. Those clarifications strengthen findings 3 and 4 as unmet conditions of this candidate, rather than accepting the report's weaker interpretation.\n\nHowever, #10806's suggested attribution \"by the window and label\" and statement that a listing pass may settle the unknown record conflict with the governing request and R-MINT-5. The candidate's adopted design explicitly says no provider label/idempotency field or completion fence is documented, and time/inventory cannot attribute a concurrent or late-applied create. This is not implementable by guessing a window. The correction must retain every unknown record until its own create answer or an actually documented completion fence, keep listings observational, and never use an invented label or inventory match to permit revocation. I asked planner and builder for that durable correction before repair follows the assertion. The stronger received-ID rule also needs consistent normative wording for R-MINT-3's failed-write clause in the repaired head. A recorded assertion does not establish an unavailable provider capability or prove these candidate defects fixed.\n\nArchitecture: In the gitseq architecture vocabulary, the head changes application-owned Git services and Room host-side execution, storage and alarm composition (the layer-4 host boundary and application behavior above it); ordinary Git remains the layer-1 evidence store. No Gitseq kernel or nexus contract (layers 2-3), interpreter binding or signature rule is changed. Inside Artroom, Workspaces is a defensible owner because it owns the fork lifecycle and the fork has no local store of its own. The fork ledger is separate from canonical MintLedger; the adapter explicitly carries it, and forkTokens has its own durable alarm candidate/backoff. The package READMEs describe those boundaries. The constructor-created tables match the existing Git service ownership pattern, and indexed due/observation access is sensible. The unresolved composition and contract conflicts above prevent architectural acceptance; merely describing an exception in plans/README.md does not amend a request or normative rule.\n\nSecurity: Provenance checks before resolving a fork, pre-send record/wake ordering, per-attempt records, safe error metadata, bounded revocation/observation, retained unknown outcomes and late-answer identity checks are valuable. The new control failures show why the complete lifecycle owner must be reviewed, including the other revoker and storage-failure path. The 600-second lifetime remains a bound on exposure under the documented provider assumption, not evidence that an unknown create has completed. I re-inspected the effective explicit fork implementation request and its canonical design bases: contract request git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:10fcfe4eb92e946549c20b66da2a915f984db91e was satisfied by the reviewed design, approval git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:ad6cc052be6922252f4793872b33a48d89957bdd is ratified, and the landing at b803d210 precedes the separate fork implementation request. The design request was contract-only; this implementation and review authorize no live credential operation or deployment. No security approval is given with the above issues unresolved.\n\nSimplification: Keep one clearly owned cleanup route per known token and one consistent expiry-classification rule across the two halves of pinning, while retaining distinct fork/canonical ownership. Sharing tested pure classification or lifecycle helpers may avoid the duplicated clock defect; a broad refactor is not required. Avoid making a second revoker infer ownership from an old inventory/keep snapshot. Preserve every requested outcome, including legitimate workspace cleanup, restart behavior and honest unknown records. Neither estimates nor the number of passing tests justify narrowing the conditions.\n\nIndependent evidence:\n- Clean detached source checkout /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v1 at 3d1223a1; separate mutation/control checkout /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v1-guards. Read all 15 changed paths, the complete fork ledger and both new suites, the report/analogy, the composed scheduling delta and protocol section 32.\n- npm ci exited 0. Root npm test: 2,408 passed, 1 skipped, exit 0. Root npm run typecheck exited 0. Git Workers: 11 passed, exit 0. Room-local Wrangler 4.146.0 deploy --dry-run exited 0; no upload. Git harness-specific typecheck/bundle were not independently repeated.\n- Eight independent source mutants all caused named runtime test failures, with no syntax/module/setup failure: wake-before-send (F1/F2/F10/F18), deleting unknown records (F4/F7/F10/F13), dropping failed revocations (F9 and others), omitting restart takeover (F7/F10/F15/F21), raising the revocation batch to 1,000 (F12), revoking from observation (F7/F8/F10/F20), dropping held tokens from the sweep keep set (F16), and storing raw revocation errors (F9). Each source was restored. The restored committed fork suite passed all 28 tests. This is a sample, not an independent rerun of the builder's 47-mutant campaign or a claim that every new guard is proved.\n- C1-C4 independently failed on restored candidate source. C3 additionally passed with the temporary ownership recheck and failed after restoration. The standalone control source is /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v1-controls.test.ts (copy beside the Git test support files to run); final output /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v1-controls-final.log; mutations /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v1-mutants.json and per-case logs; root gates /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v1-gates.log; extra gates /tmp/artroom-checker-fork-v1-extra.log.\n- All implementing commits carry the full request trailer. git diff --check passed. The source clone's merge-tree with origin/main b44601dd is clean (73e88691341d4de49d20d4f443dd63f11447b40c). Both review clones are clean; scratch tests were copied out and removed, and no candidate source change remains.\n\nNo canonical worktree edit, real credential, live provider call, deployment or claimed live smoke verification occurred. Repair the implementation defects and the confirmed ownership conditions, correct #10806's inventory/label settlement proposal without weakening unknown-record retention, preserve the full scope, and republish one integrated exact head for review. This verdict authorizes no merge.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1de0b8e5ed5f3bda593e15f1132179b04d467c22","binding":"assigned","head":"3d1223a1e06271fe6911598a716379de653e1fd6","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:994c4a8b9dd2498a9096ac9645519ecc8d3944c8\",\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:66f8b350328d0efdaca791ea0c32f7b6427d372e\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:95e84afef5a456c833de85de0eb18ada9f0c9fd0\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:66f8b350328d0efdaca791ea0c32f7b6427d372e","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}